## Why This Is Asked
This question tests ability to design practical, compliant data access controls across serverless and containerized services, with ABAC, key management, RBAC, policy-as-code, and IR planning.

## Key Concepts
- ABAC attributes and enforcement points
- MongoDB Atlas RBAC & Client-Side Encryption
- AWS KMS key management and rotation
- Policy-as-code (OPA) integration and CI gates
- Incident response timing and evidence collection

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-authorization check example
function hasAccess(attrs, request) {
  const {service, tenant, data_class, operation, region} = attrs;
  const r = request;
  return r.service === service && r.tenant === tenant && r.data_class === data_class && r.operation === operation && r.region === region;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test ABAC policies in CI/CD and simulate breaches?
- How would you audit data access for forensics and regulatory reporting?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This evaluates practical controls for vendor-integrated cloud data access and cross-service trust boundaries.

## Key Concepts
- PKCE and short-lived tokens prevent token replay and limit exposure windows
- Token binding and mTLS establish bidirectional trust between services
- Dynamic credential rotation minimizes the blast radius of leaked secrets
- Policy-as-code IAM checks enable automated, repeatable security enforcement
- Incident response playbooks ensure rapid containment and recovery

## Code Example
```javascript
const verifyPKCE = (codeChallenge, codeVerifier, method = 'S256') => {
  if (method === 'S256') {
    const hash = crypto.createHash('sha256')
      .update(codeVerifier)
      .digest('base64url');
    return hash === codeChallenge;
  }
  return codeVerifier === codeChallenge;
};

const validateTokenBinding = (token, clientCert, deviceFingerprint) => {
  return token.binding.certThumbprint === clientCert.thumbprint
    && token.binding.deviceId === deviceFingerprint.id;
};
```

Why This Is Asked
Designing robust data access for a Snowflake/MongoDB data mesh with external BI access and cross-tenant workloads is critical. The scenario requires concrete controls beyond high-level security principles, including policy-as-code, data masking, and rapid IR.

Key Concepts
- Snowflake RBAC and schema-level grants
- Dynamic data masking and row-level security
- Data classification and policy-as-code (Terraform/OPA)
- BI-tool access via OIDC, short-lived tokens, and IP allowlists
- Access History, risk alerts, and SIEM integration

Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-authorization check
if (user.tenant !== data.ownerTenant || !user.roles.includes('tenant_read')) {
  throw new Error('Access denied');
}
if (data.classification === 'PII') {
  applyMasking(data);
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you write tests to validate masking rules across datasets?
- How would you evolve policy-as-code as new datasets are added?
- How would you detect and respond to anomalous data access in real time?
